; Regression: `&b` on a borrowed reference reports `ref[Box]`, not
; `ref[ref[Box]]` (2026-08-28).
;
; The planner used to wrap the operand's type unconditionally, while the
; emitter hands the operand ITSELF back whenever there is no slot to address
; (measured shape by shape: a ref/pointer PARAMETER and a `let`-bound reference
; come out as `ret %0`, a `var` cell as `ret %r`). So `&b` claimed one wrapper
; more than the value it produced, and `type_conformance_accepted#`'s class 8
; existed to accept exactly that.
;
; ★★★ The cost of the lie was NOT in this diagnostic. Every rule that counts
;     indirection had to stay lenient by exactly the level it was counting in
;     order to survive it — and that leniency then skipped a load it owed, so
;     reading a `ref[ref[X]]` cell answered one indirection too shallow, at
;     rc 0 (ref_cell_nonoptional.scaly). **A rule that has to be inexact to
;     tolerate a wrong type is the wrong type's cost, paid somewhere else.**
;
; ★ The `var` counterpart — where `&c` IS one wrapper deeper, because a mutable
;   cell has a slot of its own — is pinned by xfail_address_of_cell_type.scaly,
;   and its RUNTIME half by address_of_optional_cell.scaly.
;
; xfail: ref[Box] where int is declared

define Box (v: i64)

function f(b: ref[Box]) returns int
{
    let n: int &b
    n
}

Console.println "`f`"
